RC(8)                          P32W Unix                           RC(8)

NAME
       rc - the system start-up script and boot sequence

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/rc

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/rc is the shell script init(8) runs, as root on the
       console, before the logins start. This page also describes
       the whole boot, from the ROM monitor to the login: prompt,
       and the way down again.

       The monitor. At reset the P32W runs the boot ROM monitor,
       which prints "P32W monitor -- ? for help" and the prompt >
       on the console (UART 0, the terminal of /dev/tty0). Its
       command b boots: it reads sector 0 of the CompactFlash card,
       the boot header, which must begin with the word "P32B"
       (0x42323350) and gives the kernel's length in sectors, its
       load address and its entry point (both 0x00100000 for P32W
       Unix). The monitor loads sectors 1 and up to that address,
       prints "loaded N sectors at ADDR" and "entry ADDR" (in hex)
       and calls the entry. A card without the header gives "not a
       boot disk". The header's fifth word is the sector where the
       filesystem starts; see fs(5).

       The kernel. It prints "P32W Unix (M4)", sets up memory, the
       process table, the buffer cache and the open-file table,
       finds the card (else it panics: "no disk") and reads the
       root filesystem's superblock (else "no filesystem on the
       disk"). If the filesystem was not marked clean at the last
       halt it prints

              warning: the filesystem was not unmounted cleanly
              (run fsck)

       It marks the filesystem in use (not clean) until the next
       halt, prints the free memory and blocks, reads the time of
       day from the real-time clock (the clock keeps UTC), starts
       the terminals and the 50 Hz clock, and creates process 1,
       which executes /etc/init.

       init and /etc/rc. init opens /dev/tty0 and runs
       /bin/sh /etc/rc there, waiting for it to finish. The /etc/rc
       that comes with the system is

              /etc/fsck -q
              : > /etc/mtab
              : > /etc/utmp
              /etc/cron
              echo P32W Unix: multi-user

       fsck -q checks the root filesystem and is silent unless
       something is wrong (it repairs nothing, and the boot goes on
       either way; repair with /etc/fsck -y). /etc/mtab and
       /etc/utmp are emptied: nothing but / is mounted, and nobody
       is logged in, at boot. cron puts itself in the background.
       Add commands here to run at each boot, for example
       /etc/mkfs /dev/ram 256 and /etc/mount /dev/ram /mnt for a
       scratch filesystem. The script runs with PATH=/bin:/usr/bin
       and HOME=/; a command that does not return (and is not put
       in the background with &) stops the boot there.

       Logins. init then starts getty(8) on tty0 and on tty1. getty
       prints "P32W Unix (tty0)" and "login:" and runs login(1) with
       the name; login checks the password (passwd(5)), records the
       login in /etc/utmp, gives the user the terminal, prints
       /etc/motd, changes to the home directory and becomes the
       user's shell. When the shell exits, init clears the utmp
       record and starts a new getty.

       Shutdown. shutdown(1) warns the terminals, sends init
       SIGTERM (no new logins), sends every other process SIGTERM
       and then SIGKILL, syncs, and calls reboot(2). The kernel
       writes out the buffer cache, marks every mounted filesystem
       clean, and halts (the machine stops; the emulator exits) or,
       with -r, restarts into the monitor, ready for b. halt(1) and
       reboot(1) do that last step at once. Switching off without
       one of them leaves the filesystem marked unclean, and the
       next boot warns.

EXAMPLES
       A boot on the console (the memory line varies):

              > b
              loaded 00000082 sectors at 00100000
              entry 00100000

              P32W Unix (M4)
              memory: ... KiB free; filesystem: ... blocks, ... free
              P32W Unix: multi-user

              P32W Unix (tty0)

              login:

FILES
       /etc/rc        the start-up script
       /etc/init      process 1
       /etc/getty     the login prompt
       /etc/fsck, /etc/cron
                      run by /etc/rc
       /etc/motd      printed at each login
       /etc/utmp      who is logged in

SEE ALSO
       init(8), getty(8), login(1), fsck(8), cron(8), mount(8),
       shutdown(1), halt(1), reboot(1), fs(5), utmp(5), motd(5)

BUGS
       There is no single-user boot to choose at the monitor: init
       goes single-user (a root shell on the console, with no
       password) only when /etc/getty is missing or not executable.
